# WARKARI
## Complete Project Development, Implementation and Deployment Report

**Project:** Warkari – Voice-First Pilgrim Assistance and Volunteer Coordination Platform  
**Technology Stack:** React, Vite, Tailwind CSS, FastAPI, Python, PostgreSQL, Supabase, Render, Vercel and Gnani AI APIs  
**Repository:** https://github.com/Manasvi-hub/Warkari  
**Frontend:** https://warkarii.vercel.app  
**Backend:** https://warkari-backend.onrender.com  
**Deployment Status:** Successfully deployed

---

# 1. Introduction

Warkari is a multilingual, voice-first pilgrim assistance and volunteer coordination platform conceptualised to support pilgrims participating in the Pandharpur Wari.

The project was developed with the objective of making essential information more accessible to pilgrims, particularly in situations where typing, navigating conventional applications, or communicating through digital interfaces may be difficult.

The platform combines voice interaction, multilingual assistance, location-related information, facility discovery, and an incident and volunteer coordination framework.

Rather than approaching Warkari as a conventional information website, the development strategy focused on creating an intelligent assistance system supported by a structured backend and a user-friendly interface.

The project was divided into six major development phases. Each phase introduced a new layer of functionality, followed by testing, debugging, and refinement.

# 2. Initial Planning and Project Roadmap

Before beginning implementation, the project was divided into phases to establish a systematic development process.

The initial roadmap focused on building the intelligence layer first, integrating voice capabilities, developing the backend infrastructure, designing the frontend, and eventually deploying the complete application.

The development roadmap was broadly organised as follows:

| Phase | Development focus |
|---|---|
| Phase 1 | Project conceptualisation, architecture and initial planning |
| Phase 2 | Gnani API integration and voice infrastructure |
| Phase 3 | Warkari Intelligence Layer and conversational refinement |
| Phase 4 | Backend architecture, database and coordination functionality |
| Phase 5 | Frontend design and application integration |
| Phase 6 | Production database migration, deployment and final verification |

The phased approach helped separate the intelligence, application, infrastructure, and deployment challenges instead of attempting to build everything simultaneously.

# 3. Phase 1 – Conceptualisation and System Planning

The first phase involved defining the problem Warkari intended to address and translating the concept into a technical architecture.

The central idea was to develop an accessible assistance platform that could communicate with pilgrims through voice rather than relying exclusively on text-based interaction.

The initial planning considered the following capabilities:

- Voice-based interaction with the platform.
- Multilingual communication.
- Information about facilities and essential services.
- Assistance with common pilgrim queries.
- A structured mechanism for reporting incidents.
- Volunteer coordination and incident status tracking.
- A frontend accessible through desktop and mobile browsers.

The architecture was divided into three principal layers:

**1. Frontend layer:** The interface through which pilgrims and authorised users interact with the system.

**2. Intelligence and service layer:** The conversational intelligence, speech processing, intent handling and response generation mechanisms.

**3. Backend and data layer:** APIs, authentication, database operations, incident management and volunteer coordination.

This separation established the foundation for the subsequent development phases.

# 4. Phase 2 – Gnani API Integration and Voice Infrastructure

The second phase focused on introducing voice capabilities.

One of the first significant challenges was obtaining and configuring the API credentials required to integrate Gnani's speech services.

The integration required understanding the API configuration, authentication requirements, request structure, response formats and how speech services would communicate with the backend.

The voice infrastructure was designed around two important capabilities:

- **Speech-to-Text (STT):** Converting a user's spoken query into text.
- **Text-to-Speech (TTS):** Converting the system's generated response into spoken audio.

The integration was not limited to making individual API requests. It was necessary to ensure that the speech services could become part of a complete conversational workflow.

This phase established the voice-processing foundation on which the Warkari Intelligence Layer would subsequently operate.

# 5. Phase 3 – Development of the Warkari Intelligence Layer

Phase 3 was one of the most challenging and important stages of the project.

The objective was to develop the Warkari Intelligence Layer: the component responsible for understanding user queries, interpreting their intent, generating contextually appropriate responses and delivering those responses through voice.

A major part of the work involved iterative refinement.

Initial responses and speech outputs did not always sound natural or appropriately contextualised. Certain words, place names and regional references were not being pronounced as intended.

Instead of accepting the initial output, the intelligence and speech-processing components were repeatedly examined and refined.

## 5.1 Conversational Intelligence

The system was developed to distinguish between different categories of user queries, including:

- General information requests.
- Facility-related queries.
- Route and location-related questions.
- Helpline-related questions.
- Emergency and incident-related requests.

An important distinction was introduced between informational helpline enquiries and actual emergency requests.

A user asking for information about a helpline should not automatically trigger incident creation.

## 5.2 Speech Normalisation and Pronunciation Refinement

One of the most interesting challenges involved making generated speech sound more relatable to the actual geographical and linguistic context of the Wari.

Generic speech synthesis does not always pronounce regional place names correctly.

To address this, dedicated pronunciation and speech-normalisation services were developed.

The implementation included contextual pronunciation substitutions for names such as:

| English representation | Marathi pronunciation |
